multiScaleR 0.7.6 (development)
---------
* Declared fixed-radius surface and landscape metrics enter analytic and Bayesian
  numeric designs with verified original normalization. Point prediction,
  snapshots, and Bayesian simulation can opt into the same fixed preparation.
  Fixed radii add no estimated bandwidth or scale prior.
  Automatic fixed reconstruction retains the original buffer extent, and legacy
  mean-only preparations without scale_vars retain their existing interfaces.
* Shared R and compiled weighted moments center their inputs before reduction.
  Weighted SQ values and scale derivatives improve stability at large additive
  origins without relaxed comparison tolerances. Finite kernel-distance estimates and
  plots remain available when Wald SEs are unavailable, with missing limits.
* Native-refit point prediction and compact snapshots share optimized spatial
  transforms, explicit site alignment, physical units, and training normalization
  across supported model classes. Verified fixed-radius normalization extends
  to recoverable native training frames.
* FFT and direct focal smoothing support the surface and categorical metric
  families. Raster means now normalize available edge support and preserve
  source NA masks; strict missing-support handling, constant weighted-SQ zero,
  stable centered moments, and RNG preservation receive focused coverage.
* Native exponential-power fits can combine means and hard-radius metrics.
  Hard radii report inapplicable shapes as NA and omit them from K, retaining
  the existing parameter layout and numerical diagnostics.
* Checked raster prediction preserves model types and explicit categorical,
  logical, and offset scenarios. Analytic GLM/NB2 SE maps use the same retained
  coefficient information as point predictions. Maps retain preparation controls
  and conditional uncertainty distinctions; posterior spatial integration is
  not replaced by a single selected-scale map.
* Marginal-effect panels preserve factors and native mixed-model response means.
  Bayesian panels use stored coefficient draws at hypothetical reference-scaled
  covariates, with explicit credible-band, random-effect, and diagnostic policies.
* AIC/BIC comparisons check recoverable responses, rows, weights, trials,
  likelihood measures, ML/REML settings, and Cox strata. Posterior targets are
  rejected. An educational feature-compatibility guide connects these contracts.
* Bayesian weighted SQ supports Gaussian/exponential kernels, fixed pooled
  reference normalization, repeated spatial IDs and matched raw-buffer prediction.
  Existing priors, posterior methods and diagnostics are preserved. Raw-cell
  differentiation has a memory preflight; private likelihood limit routines
  remain gated and no new interval calibration is claimed.
* Analytic GLM, conditional-logistic and mixed-model backends accept optimized
  weighted surface SQ with Gaussian or exponential kernels, including kernel-mean
  combinations and surface-only inputs. Prediction retains training normalization,
  row IDs and existing covariance gates; single-predictor NB2 scale refits reuse
  the same objective without claiming new interval calibration.
* A private Gaussian response-SD likelihood statistic compares fixed-SD
  profiles with a separately audited unrestricted candidate, retains negative
  discrepancies and failures, and supplies no confidence calibration.
* Consolidate likelihood and Bayesian development on one branch, with shared
  prediction contracts, probability precision diagnostics, private SD traces,
  source checks and documentation while retaining target-specific uncertainty.
* A private annual log-scale SD trace shares fixed-SD nuisance audits with the
  response-SD trace, retains exact-zero and kernel-limit distinctions, and
  supplies no global-fit eligibility, confidence cutoff or calibration claim.
* A shared inference guide distinguishes posterior and likelihood targets,
  prediction quantities, group policies, native conditioning and numerical
  precision using the existing bundled example without refitting.
* A private spatial source report checks explicit CRS, coordinate and row-ID
  agreement and nominal buffer/extent alignment. Historical source/unit lineage
  and actual kernel-weight coverage remain unverified; fitting is unchanged.
* A private Gaussian response-group SD profile trace includes exact zero and
  retains all nuisance starts, cold audits and same-SD limiting witnesses.
  It supplies no interval cutoff, global accepted-fit interface or calibration claim.
* Probability MCSE availability is separate from indicator Rhat availability.
  Balanced binary events can retain finite mean MCSE and ESS when indicator
  Rhat is undefined; the report keeps that Rhat unavailable with an explicit
  reason. Original sampling and derived-scale diagnostic gates are unchanged.
* `diagnose_bayes_probabilities()` reports chain-based probabilities and Monte
  Carlo precision at requested fitted-scale thresholds. Constant indicators,
  unavailable chains and failed diagnostics remain explicit; fit gates and
  posterior targets are unchanged.
* Prediction summaries and prepared snapshots describe their targets, included
  effects, conditioning and uncertainty through shared prediction contracts.
  Raw posterior draw outputs and native numerical predictions retain their
  existing structure; metadata does not establish eligibility or calibration.
* Marginal importance correction rechecks invalid AD target values with the
  ordinary full posterior evaluator. Valid zero posterior density no longer
  aborts solely from AD overflow. All candidates, unresolved-value failures,
  and diagnostic thresholds are retained, with explicit recovery counts.
* `predict_scales()` predicts scales at new covariates and groups, including
  future years with uncertainty in the between-year SD. Repeated scale
  combinations are summarized without a full draws-by-observations matrix.
* Bayesian `predict()` and `simulate()` accept new observations and prepared
  landscapes. Separate explicit policies handle unseen scale and response
  effects, retaining shared effects within each predicted dataset.
* New-data prediction reuses trained designs and predictor standardization.
  Grouped-binomial simulation requires explicit future trial totals; unseen
  fixed-factor coefficients remain unsupported.
* A worked prediction vignette explains future landscape scenarios, new years
  and sites, expected responses, observation intervals and joint totals.

multiScaleR 0.7.4
---------
* Marginal-effect plots for unmarked models recover predictor columns from
  component formulas, including factors and repeated site-year data. Numeric
  covariates are selected by name, categorical covariates retain their levels,
  and random effects are set to zero explicitly for conditional predictions.
* Parallel optimization now stops before screening or worker creation when the
  requested workers exceed the estimated RAM budget, with guidance for serial
  execution or eligible lean-mode inputs. RAM estimates include the binned
  summaries sent to workers. Unknown RAM estimates do not block execution.
* BREAKING REPORTING CHANGE: `kernel_dist()`, kernel plots, and the selected
  summary distance now default to the 2D radius containing the requested share
  of ideal untruncated planar kernel weight. Set `dimension = "1d"` to restore
  the original line-based definition. Fitted sigma values are unchanged.
* Summary `opt_dist` and `opt_distance` follow the selected dimension; use
  `opt_dist_1d` or `opt_dist_2d` for explicitly defined tables. Print methods
  show the selected definition first and retain the other for comparison.
* Kernel plots identify sigma, dimension, and radius or distance, and mark
  hard-radius covariates at their full buffer radius. Exponential-power 1D
  quantiles use the exact gamma formula, including long-tailed kernels.
* Simulation extents, projection windows, and stored optimization diagnostics
  retain their explicit 1D cutoff definitions.

multiScaleR 0.7.3
---------
* AIC, AICc, BIC, and sigma profiles count all free unmarked model parameters,
  including detection and variance components, using validated native metadata.
  Selection tables use explicit S4 likelihood dispatch; inconsistent metadata
  now raises an error instead of counting predictor names. AICc tables reject
  insufficient sample sizes, while profiles retain NA for undefined AICc.
* Clarify installation guidance and standardize terminology in help files and
  vignettes. Model-selection examples use bundled data with adequate sample size.

multiScaleR 0.7.2
---------
* `join_by` now preserves unmarked response and site-covariate row alignment,
  validates site keys, and rejects mismatched rows instead of recycling values.
* Optimization supports unmarked fits with component formula lists, including
  tested `pcountOpen()` and `colext()` workflows with static site covariates.
* Refit checks and sample-size diagnostics account for repeated and removed
  unmarked sites. Documentation and tests cover multi-year site mapping.

multiScaleR 0.7.1
---------
* `kernel_prep_by_group()` prepares pooled model inputs from aligned annual or
  other group-specific rasters, with shared distance bins and pooled scaling.
* Adjacency metric projection now works on non-square rasters. Optimization
  rejects candidate scales that would change the fitted sample and reports
  when the starting model already excluded prepared observations.
* Hard-radius metrics display radius rather than sigma and omit invalid
  Hessian standard errors and Wald intervals; profile intervals remain
  available on request. Profiling reports refit failures instead of returning
  an all-penalty likelihood profile.
* Raster projection supplies input layers for model offsets and accepts
  scenario values through `offset_values`. Model selection includes ancillary
  likelihood parameters such as negative-binomial dispersion in its count.
* `plot_marginal_effects()` holds offset-only variables at their sample mean
  for prediction without drawing misleading effect panels for them.
* Preparation and optimization stop early with rename guidance when a raster
  layer, derived covariate, or model predictor is named `x`, which is reserved
  for internal coordinates.
* `kernel_prep()` extracts raster cells in batches of 25 points to reduce
  temporary extraction memory while preserving point order and results.
* `kernel_dist()` gains `dimension = "2d"`, the radius of a circle holding
  `prob` of an ideal, untruncated planar kernel's weight. For a Gaussian kernel
  at 90%, it is about 2.15 sigma, versus 1.65 sigma along a line. `summary()`
  and `print()` report both distances, and `summary()` returns the planar table
  as `opt_dist_2d`;
  `opt_dist` and the `kernel_dist()` default are unchanged.

multiScaleR 0.7.0
---------
* CRAN resubmission: reduce Windows incoming check time by loading cached
  vignette objects for slow fitted-model summaries and plots. The calls used to
  regenerate those objects remain visible in the vignettes, and the cache can be
  rebuilt with `data-raw/generate_vignette_cache.R`.
* Vignettes are self-contained for CRAN rebuilds. The main guide uses package
  data and bundled example objects rather than downloading precomputed objects
  from GitHub during vignette setup.
* `profile_sigma()` is faster when profiling models with multiple expensive
  covariates. Along the profile grid only the profiled covariate's sigma
  changes, so the kernel-weighted columns of the other covariates are now
  computed once and reused, and only the profiled covariate is re-evaluated at
  each grid point. The reproducible benchmark in
  `tools/benchmarks/profile_sigma_covariate_reuse_benchmark.R` checks that the
  profile values match the legacy full-recompute loop before timing. On the
  local benchmark, the current path was 2.61x faster for four landscape metrics
  and 2.40x faster for four weighted surface metrics.
* Internal: the covariate-weighting step of `kernel_scale_fn()` is factored into
  `.msr_kernel_cov_w()` (which can compute a single covariate column), and the
  objective accepts a precomputed covariate matrix via a new `cov_w` argument.
  Behavior of the optimizer and the profile-likelihood intervals is unchanged.
* `kernel_prep()` and scale profiling now provide metric-specific guidance when
  fragile landscape metrics cannot be scaled because the derived covariate is
  all `NA` or has zero variance. The messages call out the common failure modes
  for `iji`, `siei`, and `msiei`, and the landscape-metric documentation now
  recommends prescreening these metrics before optimizing their scales.

multiScaleR 0.6.36
---------
* `landscape_var()` gains nine categorical metrics, all reproducing the values
  returned by the `landscapemetrics` package (FRAGSTATS conventions) for both the
  fixed-buffer path and the FFT raster projection.
* Configuration: `"iji"` (interspersion and juxtaposition), the evenness of the
  adjacencies among different classes. Like contagion, it is built from the
  per-window class-pair co-occurrence matrix that already powers `"contag"`, so
  it adds no extra convolutions.
* Information theory (Nowosad & Stepinski 2019): `"ent"` (marginal entropy),
  `"condent"` (conditional entropy), `"joinent"` (joint entropy), `"mutinf"`
  (mutual information), and `"relmutinf"` (relative mutual information). These
  separate composition from configuration, which the diversity and contagion
  metrics confound. The `base` argument now sets the logarithm base for these
  entropies as well as the diversity metrics; set `base = 2` for bits, matching
  `landscapemetrics`. The ratio metrics (`"relmutinf"`, `"iji"`) are unaffected
  by `base`.
* Class-level metrics, supplied through a new `class` argument naming the focal
  class: `"clumpy"` (clumpiness index, -1 to 1), `"pland"` (percentage of
  landscape), and `"ca"` (class area in hectares). `class` is required for these
  three metrics and is not accepted for any other metric.
* The landscape-metric vignette gains tables for the new families and a worked
  example contrasting composition (`"ent"`) with configuration (`"mutinf"`,
  `"clumpy"`).

multiScaleR 0.6.35
---------
* `surface_var()` gains the `"sdr"` metric (surface area ratio): the percentage
  by which the true 3D surface area of the neighborhood exceeds its flat
  projected area. It behaves as a continuous edge-density analog and uses the
  same quad triangulation as `geodiv::surface_area()`. Unlike `geodiv::sdr()`,
  which rescales the surface to a unit range and is dimensionless, multiScaleR
  computes `"sdr"` in physical units (real elevation values and the real cell
  size), so a tilted plane of slope `s` returns `(sqrt(1 + s^2) - 1) * 100`.
  The physical definition is the interpretable one for terrain and, unlike the
  rescaled version, is FFT-projectable.
* `"sdr"` joins `"sdq"` as a neighbor-based surface metric (it caches raster
  cell IDs and its projection matches point extraction to a small boundary
  tolerance). Kernel weighting is not offered for the slope/area family.

multiScaleR 0.6.34
---------
* `surface_var()` gains a `weighted` argument. With `weighted = TRUE`, the
  distribution metrics (`"sa"`, `"sq"`, `"ssk"`, `"sku"`) are computed as
  kernel-weighted moments: each cell is weighted by the model's distance kernel
  (gaussian, exp, or expow) and the optimized parameter is the kernel scale
  (sigma) rather than a hard neighborhood radius. Because the weights vary
  smoothly with sigma, the likelihood is smooth in the scale parameter, so
  `multiScale_optim()` estimates the scale of effect of heterogeneity with a
  standard error, exactly as it does for a kernel-weighted mean. The default
  (`weighted = FALSE`) is unchanged.
* Weighted surface metrics are supported with every kernel, including
  `"expow"`, since they use the kernel (and its shape). Weighting is not
  available for the slope metric `"sdq"`, and the scale is always optimized
  (no fixed `radius`).
* Projection of weighted `"sq"`, `"ssk"`, and `"sku"` uses kernel-weighted FFT
  power sums; weighted `"sa"` uses the compiled focal pass, now generalized to
  accept per-cell weights (a 0/1 mask reproduces the unweighted result exactly).
* Added a vignette section contrasting hard-radius and kernel-weighted
  roughness.

multiScaleR 0.6.33
---------
* `surface_var()` gains three metrics: `"ssk"` (skewness) and `"sku"`
  (excess kurtosis), which describe the shape of the neighborhood value
  distribution, and `"sdq"` (root mean square slope), which describes local
  gradient steepness or ruggedness. With `"sa"` and `"sq"` this completes the
  dispersion, distribution-shape, and slope families of gradient surface
  metrics. `"ssk"` and `"sku"` match `geodiv`'s adjusted-skewness and
  excess-kurtosis defaults; `"sdq"` matches `geodiv::sdq()` after accounting
  for cell resolution (multiScaleR reports a true slope, dividing the gradient
  by the cell size).
* Raster projection of `"ssk"`, `"sku"`, and `"sdq"` uses FFT convolution. The
  moment projections center on the global raster mean first to avoid
  floating-point cancellation in the third and fourth power sums. Like the
  landscape edge metrics, `"sdq"` caches raster cell IDs and its projection
  agrees with point extraction to within a small boundary tolerance.
* Added the `surface_metric_covariates` vignette section demonstrating the
  shape and slope surfaces.

multiScaleR 0.6.32
---------
* Added `surface_var()`, a new covariate specification for continuous surface
  texture metrics, alongside the existing `kernel_var()` and `landscape_var()`.
  Two metrics are supported in this release: `"sa"` (average roughness, the mean
  absolute deviation from the neighborhood mean) and `"sq"` (root mean square
  roughness, the sample standard deviation of the neighborhood values). Both
  definitions match the `geodiv` package. These metrics summarize
  within-neighborhood heterogeneity of a continuous raster (elevation, NDVI,
  canopy height, temperature, and similar surfaces) rather than its mean, so
  they complement `kernel_var()`. The buffer radius can be fixed or optimized,
  and the covariates flow through `kernel_prep()`, `multiScale_optim()`, and
  `kernel_scale.raster()` exactly like landscape metrics.
* `surface_var()` projections in `kernel_scale.raster()` compute `"sq"` with
  fast Fourier transform (FFT) convolution and `"sa"` with an exact masked focal
  pass. Average roughness has no closed-form FFT decomposition, so `"sa"` is
  projected with a compiled focal routine (`surface_sa_focal_cpp`) rather than
  an R closure; this is exact and roughly twice as fast as the equivalent
  `terra::focal()` call in benchmarks. Surface metrics require a continuous
  raster with square cells.
* Added `geodiv` to `Suggests`; the surface-metric tests use it as a validation
  oracle and are skipped when it is not installed.
* Added the `surface_metric_covariates` vignette, which walks through defining,
  fitting, optimizing, and projecting surface texture covariates and how to
  interpret roughness at different scales.

multiScaleR 0.6.31
---------
* `multiScale_optim()` no longer queries system RAM on every call. The
  `next_run` recommendation previously invoked `estimate_multiscale_ram()` at
  the end of each fit, which shelled out to an external process (PowerShell on
  Windows) and rebuilt the optimization context; under load this could make
  `multiScale_optim()` appear to hang after the optimization had finished. The
  parallel worker-count suggestion is now obtained only on demand by calling
  `estimate_multiscale_ram()` directly. `next_run$n_cores` is now `NULL`.
  `estimate_multiscale_ram()`'s own RAM query is also bounded by a 5-second
  timeout so it degrades to `NA` instead of blocking.
* `kernel_prep()` now precomputes distance-binned summaries of kernel-weighted
  covariates (`bin = TRUE`, `nbins = 256` by default). `multiScale_optim()`,
  `profile_sigma()`, and the screened-start path evaluate kernel covariates from
  these summaries in O(nbins) per point instead of iterating every buffer cell
  on each optimizer evaluation. The per-evaluation cost becomes independent of
  `max_D`, making optimization roughly 10-80x faster in benchmarks (the speedup
  grows with the number of points and the buffer size), while recovering sigma
  to within ~1e-5 (relative) of the exact per-cell result. Set `bin = FALSE` to
  force the exact path.
* Added a "lean" storage mode (`kernel_prep(store_cell_data = FALSE)`) that
  drops the per-cell `d_list`/`raw_cov` data when every covariate is
  kernel-type, retaining only the binned summaries. This reduces the
  `multiScaleR_data` memory footprint by roughly 30-40x in benchmarks and is
  sufficient to optimize, profile, and refit. Cell-level data are retained
  automatically when landscape-metric covariates are present.
* `estimate_multiscale_ram()` and `print.multiScaleR_data()` now report binned
  summary sizes and handle lean inputs.
* `kernel_prep()` now stores each point's extracted cell values as a dense or
  sparse matrix, whichever is smaller (continuous and categorical layers store
  dense; low-prevalence binary layers stay sparse), so `raw_cov` memory is never
  worse than before and is ~1.5x smaller for continuous rasters. `scale_type()`
  evaluates dense values in vectorized R, matching the compiled sparse path to
  machine precision while avoiding per-cell sparse random access; this speeds up
  the exact (`bin = FALSE`) per-cell weighting and removes the repeated
  sparse-to-dense conversion in the landscape-metric path. (One-time
  `kernel_prep()` and landscape optimization wall-clock change little, as those
  are dominated by raster extraction and the C++ metric computation
  respectively.)
* Landscape-metric optimization is ~3.4-4.2x faster: the source raster's
  categorical encoding is validated once in `kernel_prep()` instead of being
  re-scanned (with a per-call `unique()` class detection) on every optimizer
  evaluation. Optimized radii are unchanged. Direct calls to the landscape
  metric helpers still validate by default.
* Hardware detection (physical/logical cores and total system RAM) is now
  memoized, so `multiScale_optim()` no longer spawns a PowerShell process to
  query RAM on every call when populating its parallel-worker recommendation.

